                  <msItem>
                     <title>Lives of St Thomas the Martyr etc.</title>
                     <note type="MLGB3_generalNotes">A. G. Watson, Dated and Datable Manuscripts in
                        the British Library (Oxford, 1979), no. 429 and pl. 401.</note>
                     <msItem>
                        <title>(1) Customary of the shrine of St Thomas Becket (drawn up in
                           1428)</title>
                     </msItem>
                     <msItem>
                        <title>(2) and (3) Lives of St Thomas (s. xiii ex).</title>
                     </msItem>
                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="1250/1500" notBefore="1250" notAfter="1500" calendar="#gregorian">s. xiii ex--xv</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_4">Canterbury, Kent. Benedictine
                        cathedral priory of Holy Trinity or Christ Church</orgName><p>According to
                        the BL DigCIM website:</p><p>James of Dover (d. 1397) and Thomas Elys (d.
                        c.1409), guardians of the shrine of Thomas Becket: a list of the masses
                        celebrated by them at the shrine and an inventory of ecclesiastical
                        furnishings and vestments sent to Canterbury College Oxford, both referring
                        to 1380, with the date of February 1426 (fol. 141v); inscribed 'Omnibus
                        dilectis sancte Cantuar' eccl[es]ie confratribus presentibus et futuris
                        confratres Johannes Vyel et Edmundus Kyngyston Feretarii sive Custodes
                        famosi secretarii sive custodes ... [one line erased] ... Millesimo
                        quadringentesimo vicesimo octavo ...' (fol. 1r). All three parts of the
                        volume were probably owned by the guardians of the shrine by this date, as
                        in the third text, Guernes' Vie (fol. 136r, l. 5), there is a reference to
                        this verse life being recited to visiting pilgrims at the shrine, 'E meinte
                        fez li dist a la tunbe al barun'.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Joseph Haslewood, lot 546 in the sale of 8
                     May 1809.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Richard Heber, lot 322 in the sale of 10
                     February 1836; sold to Payne.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Sir Thomas Phillipps (1792-1872), his no.
                     8113.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Sold at Sotheby's, 26 November 1975 as lot
                     826 to A. G. Thomas for £7,500.</provenance>
               </history>
